/* Short notes on well-known stars, checked against published sources. */
window.SKYSTARNOTES={"32349":"The brightest star in the night sky, helped by being one of the nearest, at 8.6 light-years. It has a faint white-dwarf companion, Sirius B, which circles it every 50.1 years.","37279":"Like Sirius, it has a faint white-dwarf companion, Procyon B, which orbits it every 40.8 years.","69673":"In 1933 its light was used to switch on the lights of Chicago's Century of Progress world's fair. The light was thought to have set out 40 years earlier, at the time of the city's previous world's fair in 1893; the star is actually about 37 light-years away.","91262":"The first star ever photographed, at Harvard College Observatory on the night of 16–17 July 1850, and the first whose spectrum was photographed, by Henry Draper in 1872. Because Earth's axis slowly wobbles, Vega will be the pole star around AD 13,700, as it was around 12,000 BC.","24608":"Not one star but two yellow giants that orbit each other every 104 days, with a distant pair of red dwarfs. The figures here are for their combined light.","27989":"A red supergiant: put in place of the Sun, it would swallow the orbits of Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars. From late 2019 to early 2020 it faded to magnitude 1.6 (the 'Great Dimming'), which was traced to a cloud of dust it had thrown off.","97649":"It spins once every 7.8 hours (the Sun takes about 25 days), so fast that it is visibly flattened, its equator bulging outward.","49669":"It spins once in about 16 hours, so fast that it is flattened at the poles and bulges at the equator.","7588":"The flattest star known: it spins so fast that its equatorial diameter is far larger than its polar diameter.","65474":"Two hot blue stars orbiting each other every 4.01 days, so close together that each is pulled into an egg shape.","37826":"The nearest giant star to the Sun. A planet, named Thestias, was announced in 2006, but studies in 2013 and 2021 questioned whether it is really there.","36850":"Six stars in one system: three pairs, all bound together by gravity.","11767":"The North Star, within a degree of the celestial pole; it will be closest to the pole around the year 2100. It is a triple star, and its brightest member is the nearest Cepheid, the kind of pulsating star used to measure cosmic distances.","14576":"The 'Demon Star' (its name comes from Arabic for the demon or ghoul). An eclipsing binary: every 2.87 days the dimmer companion passes in front of the brighter star, and Algol fades from magnitude 2.1 to 3.4 during an eclipse lasting about ten hours. John Goodricke measured the cycle in 1782 and suggested eclipses as the cause.","10826":"'The Wonderful': the first variable star recognised, apart from novae; David Fabricius noticed it in 1596. Over about 332 days it swings from as bright as magnitude 2 down to about 10, far below naked-eye visibility.","68756":"The pole star of the pyramid builders: it was the star nearest the pole from about 3900 to 1800 BC, and closest to it in 2830 BC.","80763":"Its name means 'rival of Ares', the rival of Mars, which it resembles in colour. A red supergiant with a hot companion star; the figures here are for their combined light.","71683":"The nearest star system to the Sun. Its two bright stars, A and B, orbit each other about every 80 years; a third, the faint red dwarf Proxima Centauri, is the closest star of all, at 4.25 light-years, and has at least two planets.","71681":"The nearest star system to the Sun. Its two bright stars, A and B, orbit each other about every 80 years; a third, the faint red dwarf Proxima Centauri, is the closest star of all, at 4.25 light-years, and has at least two planets.","113368":"Surrounded by belts of dust and debris imaged by Hubble, ALMA and the James Webb Space Telescope. An object first thought to be a planet, 'Fomalhaut b', turned out to be an expanding cloud of dust.","65378":"One of the first double stars seen through a telescope; with Alcor beside it, it also makes a famous naked-eye pair.","65477":"Mizar's faint companion. Picking it out with the naked eye was an old test of eyesight.","21421":"It looks as if it belongs to the Hyades star cluster, but at about 65 light-years it is less than half as far away; it just happens to lie in front."};
